Hello All,

Background for selling with respect to questions on DI Nipple exposed threa=
ds:


1.       In selling with respect to this issue the idea is to use the facts=
 and logic already in place with a customer as follows...

a.       The fact that everyone's system has DI components present in the w=
ay of pipe, fittings, and valves.

b.      They will have had to grapple with the potential for corrosion and =
measures if it is present.

c.       The DI nipples are just another DI fitting that may or may not req=
uire protective measures depending on their circumstances.

d.      Exposed threads are no more or less vulnerable than simple bitumino=
us coated DI pipe and fittings.

e.      Whatever they have been doing for DI pipe and fittings is what they=
 will have to do for DI nipples exposed threads.

2.       Other relevant information.

a.       The notion that exposed threads are relatively susceptible to corr=
osion stems from the false perception that standard bituminous coating prov=
ides protection.  Bituminous coating standard on DI pipe and fittings as we=
ll as our nipples provides no corrosion protection.

b.      The thread area is thinner than the other areas so if corrosion pro=
cesses are present they will tend to fail first.

c.       If corrosion is possible protective measures should be taken regar=
dless of thickness.  Thickness is not an appropriate protection measure.

d.      In general Ductile Iron Pipe Research Association (DIPRA) guideline=
s should be followed.

3.       Scenarios:

a.       If the soils are not corrosive then no protection of the exposed t=
hreads is required

b.      If the soils are corrosive then protection of these exposed threads=
 is required.

                                                               i.      If t=
he customer has corrosive soils then he is probably prescribing some protec=
tion.

1.       If that protection is polyethylene encasement then the customer's =
current method would apply and not change.

2.       If that protection is factory applied FBE then the exposed threads=
 will require additional measures such as encasement or some other field ap=
plied protection system

a.       Recall that our FBE of nipples is more about a lining for the insi=
de of the nipple since cement lining is not feasible.

3.       If that protection is cathodic protection then the customer's curr=
ent method would apply and not change.

We have looked at thin protective coating possibilities that would be suita=
ble for threads but have not developed them.  FBE over the threads will lea=
d to issues with making the joints and may ultimately not protect the threa=
ds consistently since the coating will be disrupted by the assembly process=
.

John


